# Nature Watch

- The related repositories are `marynvdl/naturewatch-app` (web application) and `marynvdl/naturewatch-analysis` (data processing and publication). Identify which repository a change belongs in. Another repository's URL does not mean its files are available locally.
- Read the relevant README and code before changing behavior. For architecture or pipeline changes, read the relevant sections of `docs/blueprint.md`. Layer facts for readers live in `docs/data.md` and `docs/using-the-map.md`. If a cited doc is missing, report that instead of inventing it.
- Keep accepted architecture, pipeline contracts and bucket roles in the blueprint. Keep ideas and open questions in Notion; actionable work lives in Todoist. Do not assume access to either service unless an integration is explicitly available.
- For the app, branch feature and bugfix work from `develop`; release through a pull request into `main`. Check the analysis repository's own branch conventions before applying this workflow there.
- Follow the blueprint's public-serving versus analytical-storage policy. Do not infer access from bucket names or change access policies without authorization.
- Inspect workflow triggers before pushing: a push or merge may launch processing or deploy. Before triggering billable processing, provisioning cloud resources, or deploying production, explain the action and expected cost or impact and obtain approval unless that exact action is already authorized.
- Authorized local edits and relevant local checks may proceed without repeated confirmation.
- Before pushing or opening a pull request, run the relevant test suite and confirm it passed. For the app, that is the catalog vitest suite when the catalog or frontend changed, and the Test suite workflow on the pull request before treating the change as ready. A push is not a passing test.
- Long Earth Engine jobs must run independently of the chat. After an authorized submission, record the task ID, output location, status-check method and cancellation method. Report and return rather than repeatedly polling or waiting for completion. Never equate submission with success.

## Documentation

After completing a feature or significant technical change, review the relevant documentation and update only what the change affects:

- `docs/blueprint.md`: current architecture, data flow, storage, deployment, important behavior and accepted design direction. Keep it concise. Distinguish implemented behavior from agreed plans. Revise existing sections rather than appending a chronological account of changes.
- `docs/blueprint-notes.md`: useful technical detail, verification evidence and significant processing-run records. Preserve dates and versions on historical evidence; distinguish it from current facts. Do not accumulate routine edits, full chat transcripts or repeated implementation summaries.
- `docs/data.md`: layer sources, observation periods, units, methods, coverage, legends and limitations.
- `docs/using-the-map.md`: user-visible controls, workflows and behavior, including year fallback where relevant.

Verify documentation against the changed code and available evidence. Flag unverifiable claims instead of inventing facts. Include relevant documentation changes in the same commit or pull request as the implementation.

In the completion summary, state which documentation was updated, or briefly explain why no update was needed. Report checks performed, remaining uncertainty and any jobs still running.

## Git identity and attribution

- The repositories belong to GitHub account `marynvdl`.
- Preserve my existing Git author name and email configuration.
- Do not add AI or tool attribution to commit messages or pull request titles and descriptions.
- This includes `Co-authored-by: Cursor`, `Made-with: Cursor`, `Made with Cursor`, and equivalent attribution for other AI tools.
- Preserve attribution to genuine human contributors.
- Follow the project's existing release-version naming convention.
- Check the resulting commit message and pull request description for unwanted tool attribution before considering the work complete.